00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.260
Rhett: Alright, so what I'm doing right now is I'm spacing all of the feet 16 inches apart

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.320
And I'm pre-drilling holes in it so I don't have to switch the bit every time.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:19.180
Then I'm gonna take the wood screws and go all the way down, and the base board will be complete.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.420
Once we have these going up all the way down the wall, we should be able to fit about 65 guns going down

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.800
Which...should be enough?

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:29.860
Sam: What's up?

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.960
We're going back to the tried and true method of doing a 3D scan with the Kinect.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.700
It's always handy to have a 3D model of somebody for a video to shoot.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:41.140
3D scanning people is actually incredibly easy. You just need a good program and a Kinect.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.320
We're using an application called "Ska-nect"

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.020
Plug it in, make sure it's all working, aim it at your subject, hit record.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.900
Basically, you can scan it around, move it around, or in our case, just have the subject spin in place.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.860
(To wren) Also kinda keep your hands out to your sides instead of out like this, yeah.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.220
Rhett: More than enough room for the guns.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:07.600
Based on the amount that I fit in these three slots right here, I think we're doing pretty darn good when it comes to gun enclosures.

